---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DNS TTL Explained: What TTL Means and Best Values"
  description="DNS TTL explained: what time-to-live means, how it controls caching and propagation, and recommended TTL values for stability and migrations."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">TTL Explained</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DNS TTL Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      TTL, time-to-live, is the number of seconds a resolver may keep serving your cached answer
      before asking again. It is the single dial that trades <strong class="text-ink">speed of
      change</strong> against <strong class="text-ink">speed of resolution</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        When a resolver fetches <code>example.com A 203.0.113.10</code> with TTL 3600, it will answer
        that value from cache for the next hour without contacting your nameserver. Change the record
        and the world updates not instantly, but as each cache's copy expires. That lag is
        <a href="/dns-propagation/">propagation</a>, and TTL is its clock.
      </p>

      <h2>Recommended values</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Stable records</strong> (a site you never move): 3600 (1 hour) to 86400 (1 day). Fewer queries, faster answers.</li>
        <li><strong>Normal operations:</strong> 3600 is the sensible default most providers ship.</li>
        <li><strong>Planned change:</strong> drop to 300 (5 min) at least one old-TTL cycle before the change, then raise it back after.</li>
        <li><strong>Never:</strong> TTL 0. It disables caching and hammers your nameservers for no benefit.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The migration mistake everyone makes</h2>
      <p>
        Changing a record while its old TTL is 86400 means up to a day of mixed traffic hitting the
        old server. The fix is ordering: lower the TTL first, wait out the old value, then change the
        record, watch the <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a> until resolvers
        agree, then restore the TTL.
      </p>

      <h2>Check your TTLs</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/dns-record-lookup/">record lookup</a> shows the TTL of every answer, live. If
        your records still show 300 from a migration you did months ago, raise them.
      </p>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/dns-propagation/">DNS propagation explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/how-dns-works/">How DNS works</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
